Fillets: two roundings of a face narrower than both whose balls cannot
make one rounding over it (mitcad#121). The second rounding rolls on the
first one's contact line on that face, and the face vanishes.

0013 computes two stripes whose contacts on a face between their edges
each lie beyond the other's again, overflowing that face across each
other's edge, so that both make one blend tangent to the faces beyond.
Where no ball touches those faces (a plate thinner than the diameter of
the roundings of both its rims), that computation fails and so did the
fillet. Now, for two roundings of the same kind (both convex or both
concave), ChFi3d_Builder::RideOnOverlap (from Compute):

- The earlier stripe (in the builder's order) is computed again on the
  faces of its edge, as without the overlap.
- The later stripe rolls on the earlier one's contact line on the face:
  its start (StartSolOverflow) is a ball touching its other face and
  passing through that line, set up as a blend rolling on an edge (an
  obstacle), the line given as an edge whose p-curves lie on the face and
  on the earlier blend's surface (a face made of that surface); the
  earlier blend holds the line as the face beyond an obstacle edge does.
  The analytic blends (KPart) are not used for it. Its common points at
  the ends of the line lie on the arcs the earlier one's do (the line's
  direction there reversed, as the later blend sees it).
- After the corners, ChFi3d_Builder::ApplyRides: the two blends meet on
  that line. Both stripes' sides there hold the earlier one's contact
  curve, each with its p-curve on its own surface, and lie on no face, as
  the sides of a fillet end do; the later stripe's points on the line are
  the earlier one's. The face has no contact line left and vanishes; the
  arcs at the ends of the line, between that face and the faces at the
  ends, are cut at their vertices, as ChFi3d_CutConsumedFaces cuts the
  edges of a face a blend rolling on an edge consumes, and the blends'
  points on them become mere points.
- PerformOneCorner: the end cap (cork) of a blend whose p-curve on the
  face at the end meets an existing p-curve there only at the cork's end
  points (the caps of the two blends meet where the line ends) is no
  crossing of fillets.
- ChFi3d_StripeEdgeInter does not compare the contacts of two stripes on
  the sides that lie on that line (face index 0) as if on a common face.
- ChFi3d_MakeExtremities and PerformSetOfKGen enlarge the boxes of common
  points on arcs with the faces of those arcs; an arc that is no edge of
  the shape (that line) has none.
